ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Endometriosis Central Sensitisation Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ovarian torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral, localized adnexal mass tenderness, and lack of acute, sudden-onset surgical rigidity.

Ruptured ectopic pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure without signs of hemorrhagic shock, and patient reports reliable use of continuous hormonal contraception.

Acute appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Diffuse bilateral pelvic pain rather than migration to the right lower quadrant, and absence of fever or rebound tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of unremitting, severe pelvic pain with autonomic signs (nausea, tachycardia) mandated immediate in-person evaluation to rule out acute surgical emergencies such as ovarian torsion or visceral rupture. Once a benign surgical abdomen was confirmed, the severity of the central sensitization flare required potent, immediate-acting intramuscular analgesics to break the pain cycle. Withholding treatment until the next in-hours period would have necessitated an emergency department transfer for an established chronic pain exacerbation.

